      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The scanner paired a same-origin network primitive with a presumed dynamic-execution sink inside one very broad file span. Tracing actual flows: the extension reads its own settings field from character cards and chat metadata through the host's character, settings, and chat endpoints, and temporarily wraps the page's fetch function to serialize chat saves behind a Web Locks lock and to preserve or separate its per-character image settings when characters are imported, deleted, or renamed. Every wrapped call is restricted to the page's own origin and delegates to the original function, which is restored on cleanup. All responses are parsed as JSON and passed through a sanitizer that keeps only validated path segments plus list, directory, and random-pick fields, used purely as configuration and image-selection data. No network-derived content reaches any dynamic code, command, or script-injection sink in the supplied range. The interception and persistence match the extension's stated purpose of managing extra avatar images across character lifecycle events and are disclosed in code comments.",
      "layman_explanation": "This is a pattern match, not a real danger sign. The extension only communicates with the same SillyTavern app it runs in, never with outside services, to load and save its own image settings. It briefly watches the app's own web requests so it can tidy up its image lists when characters are imported, deleted, or renamed, and it restores normal behavior when it is done. Nothing it downloads is ever run as a program; it is just settings data that gets checked before being used to pick pictures.",
